  "query_preview": "..\nToday: The weeks closed Low will push north as a upper level \ntrough dips in from the Gulf of Alaska. The Low center will pass\nover Western MT and swing a cold front through the Inland \nNorthwest during the morning hours. Rain chances will diminish \nwest to east as the front passes. Short term ensembles have a \n25-75 probability of an additional 0.1-0.3 of an inch of precip \nthrough Friday. Most will fall through the morning hours. As the\nbackside of the trough moves into the region, a low level jet \nof 50-60 kts will bring push breezy north to south low level \nwinds through the Okanogan Valley and across the Western \nColumbia Basin. Wind speeds in the low to mid level (800-750mb) \nare in the 30-40 kt range. Surface winds in aforementioned areas\ncan expect sustained winds in the upper teens-20 mph and gusts \ninto the upper 30-40 mph range. The Waterville Plateau has a \nnear 50 percent probability of isolated gusts into the 50s. A \nwind advisory has been issued for the areas of concern. Winds \nwill start to decrease mid afternoon as A ridge starts to fill \nin behind the exiting trough. Highs will be in the 50s to low \n60s. Overnight lows will be 20s to low 30s.\n\nSaturday through Thursday: Saturday will be be mainly dry as \nthe region is under a dry slot between the exiting Low and \nanother one located off the coast of BC. Models continue to show\na low dropping along the coast. The discrepancy comes with the \ntrack of the low. Around 60 percent of the ensembles have the \nLow pushing through the North Oregon and bringing another round \nof light precip to the region. The other 40 percent have the Low\nfizzling out along the coast line with a stronger ridge over \nthe region. Highs for the period will be in the upper 40s and \n50s. Overnight lows will be in the upper 20s and 30s. /JDC",
